 grouping the kinase structures into four well-deﬁned conformations ( Figure 2 ; CIDI, CIDO, CODI, and CODO), as well as the uCD conformation ( Figure 3 ). We showed that representing kinase structures with descriptors derived from the aC helix and the DFG motif, such as cg_vec and D1/ D2vectors, respectively, can capture movements that are asso- ciated with the kinase catalytic activity and inhibitor-binding mode ( Figure S5 ). Unexpectedly, our results also show that fea- tures derived from some structural motifs (e.g., the R-spine) donot contribute to effective classiﬁcation of the conformational states. Describing the Conformational Space of Protein Kinases The distribution of kinase structures among the ﬁve conforma- tions in all kinase families is uneven ( Figure 4 B). The majority of the structures are solved in the active CIDI conformation. ",
